[2026-10-04T16:42:59.030Z] [INFO]         "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. **Primary request and intent**\n   - Solve GitHub issue https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107.\n   - Working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791131286882. Branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b. Draft PR #108 already exists; update it with `gh pr edit` and do not create a new PR.\n   - Push only to issue-107-bce58c21006b. Make atomic commits. Run local checks before pushing.\n   - When finished: merge main into the branch if needed, make sure CI passes, update the PR description in the style of merged PR #106, then run `gh pr ready 108`.\n   - Put experiment scripts in ./experiments and usage examples in ./examples.\n   - Git user is konard. The user's email (link.assistant.team@proton.me) may be used only to identify the user, such as for authorship; it must never be sent to unrelated services.\n\n   **Issue #107 requirements** (the issue has no comments):\n   - Generate the README results in exactly this hierarchy:\n     ```\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for links\n       Rust doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n       C# doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for objects\n       (same Rust/C# × 32/64-bit structure)\n     ```\n   - The SQLite table must be `links(id, from, to)`, with id typed as int or bigint consistently between C# and Rust, and with obvious indices on id, from and to.\n   - Coverage must be fully symmetric across languages and bit widths.\n   - Both languages need object benchmarks with a sequences cache and without one.\n   - Update all dependencies to their latest versions.\n   - Code should be self-explanatory: few comments, good names, short, using the most common ways to use SQLite and Doublets.\n   - No false positives or false negatives.\n   - Fit the largest sizes possible (100k, 1M, 10M, 100M) within 6-hour CI jobs, splitting jobs per benchmark if needed. Use fewer repetitions at larger sizes so the total work is roughly constant, following benchmarking best practices.\n   - \"Make it perfect.\"\n\n2. **Key technical concepts**\n   - **Rust, stable toolchain (verified)**\n     - doublets 0.5.0 builds on stable rustc 1.98.1 (requires 1.85+), so the nightly pin `nightly-2022-08-22` and `#![feature(allocator_api)]` can be dropped.\n     - Store types: `doublets::{unit, split, mem::{Global, FileMapped}, Doublets, DoubletsExt}`; `unit::Store::<T,_>::new(Global::new())` or `with_constants(mem, LinksConstants::external())`.\n     - Methods: `create`, `create_point`, `create_link`, `update`, `delete`, `get_link`, `each_iter([any,s,t])`, `search`, `single`, `get_or_create`, `count`, `delete_all`.\n     - No existence validation: links may reference ids that do not exist yet.\n   - **Rust dependency to add**\n     - platform-data 2.0.0 (add it as a renamed direct dependency, e.g. `platform-data`). It provides `LinksConstants` (`external()` gives internal `1..=MAX/2` and external `MAX/2..=MAX`), `AddrToRaw`, `RawToAddr`, `Hybrid` and `LinkReference`.\n     - `LinkReference` comes from platform-num 0.8 and supports TryFrom/TryInto for all integer types plus `from_byte`.\n   - **Latest versions**\n     - Rust: rusqlite 0.40.2 (bundled), criterion 0.8.2 (possibly dropped), once_cell 1.21.4.\n     - NuGet: Platform.Data.Doublets 0.18.1, Platform.Data.Doublets.Sequences 0.6.5, Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.12, BenchmarkDotNet 0.15.8 (possibly dropped), EF Core 10.0.12 (to be dropped).\n     - Target net10.0, since the local SDK is 10.0.112. global.json needs updating.\n   - **C# Sequences idioms**\n     - Symbol = `GetOrCreate(AddressToRawNumber(char), unicodeSymbolMarker)`.\n     - String = `GetOrCreate(BalancedVariant(symbols), unicodeSequenceMarker)`; `BalancedVariantConverter` repeatedly halves the list by pairwise GetOrCreate.\n     - `CachingConverterDecorator` wraps the string→sequence and sequence→string converters; this is the \"sequences cache\".\n     - Reading uses `RightSequenceWalker` with `TargetMatcher(symbolMarker)`.\n     - `PropertiesOperator.SetValue(obj, prop, val)` = `GetOrCreate(obj, prop)`, then `DeleteMany(AllIndices(any, objectProperty))`, then `GetOrCreate(objectProperty, val)`.\n     - `PropertiesOperator.GetValue` = `SearchOrDefault(obj, prop)`, then the target of `SingleOrDefault(any, objectProperty, any)`.\n     - The C# long→raw-number-sequence converter looks buggy, so dates will be stored as a single raw number (unix seconds below 2^31, valid for both widths).\n     - Neither the C# memory `Update` nor the Rust store translates `Itself`. Markers are created as `root = CreatePoint(); m = create(); update(m, root, m)`.\n   - **Planned architecture (decided in reasoning, not yet implemented)**\n     - A small custom harness, identical in Rust and C#, replacing Criterion, BenchmarkDotNet and EF Core. Justification: symmetry, control of repetitions, reuse of state, and the 6-hour budget.\n     - Each repetition runs a full lifecycle on a fresh store: create N (timed) → query_all, query_by_id, query_by_from_to, query_by_from, query_by_to (each timed) → update N (timed; swaps from/to) → delete N (timed) → drop and remove files.\n     - Warm-up repetition at size min(N, 100k), discarded.\n     - R = max(1, W/N) with W configurable through an environment variable.\n     - Report median, min and max ns/op.\n     - Every timed batch returns count and checksum, validated against the expected values (self-verifying, guarding against false results).\n   - **Links dataset** (ids 1..N, deterministic in both languages)\n     - `splitmix64(x)`: z = x + 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15; z = (z ^ (z >> 30)) * 0xBF58476D1CE4E5B9; z = (z ^ (z >> 27)) * 0x94D049BB133111EB; return z ^ (z >> 31).\n     - `from(i) = 1 + splitmix64(2i) % i`, `to(i) = i + 1 − from(i)`. Pairs are unique by construction and references are always ≤ i.\n     - Checksum Σ(id + from + to) = N² + 2N for every query operation, and each query returns count N.\n     - Point operations (by_id, by_from, by_to, by_from_to, update, delete) visit ids in a stride permutation `1 + (k*STRIDE) % N`, with STRIDE coprime to N (derived from 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15 % N, adjusted until gcd = 1).\n   - **SQLite**\n     - Schema: `CREATE TABLE links (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, \"from\" INTEGER NOT NULL, \"to\" INTEGER NOT NULL)`; indexes on `(\"from\",\"to\")` and `(\"to\",\"from\")`, equivalent to Doublets' sources and targets trees.\n     - The rowid serves as the id index. The same schema is used for both widths, because SQLite integer storage is variable-length; this must be explained in the PR.\n     - One transaction per timed batch (reads included); prepared statements reused (rusqlite `prepare_cached`; C# reused `SqliteCommand` with parameters).\n   - **Objects**\n     - Blog post fields: title, content (ASCII pseudo-random words from a lorem vocabulary), publication date (unix seconds).\n     - Variants: SQLite_Memory, SQLite_File, and Doublets {United, Split} × {Volatile, NonVolatile} × {cache on, cache off}.\n     - Operations: create, read_all, read_by_id, delete. Delete removes the object link plus its property and value links; shared sequences remain.\n     - SQLite table: blog_posts(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title, content, publication date INTEGER), with no secondary indices.\n     - Doublets layout: post = (blog_post_marker, self); properties = (object, marker) → (property, value).\n   - **CI plan**\n     - A single benchmarks workflow with a matrix over language × width × category × size (split per variant at huge sizes if needed), each job ≤ 6h.\n     - Jobs upload JSON results; a final report job (`if: always()`) merges them with the committed results JSON and regenerates the README EN and RU sections in the required hierarchy, plus charts.\n     - rust.yml and CI.yml keep build, test and smoke-run jobs.\n\n3. **Files and code sections**\n   - No repository files have been modified yet. The working tree is clean at commit 12eccc9.\n   - **Existing repository files** (from the prior summary):\n     - rust/{Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, rust-toolchain.toml, rustfmt.toml, .gitignore, out.py, test_out.py, results.md}\n     - rust/src/{lib.rs, fork.rs, exclusive.rs, objects.rs, sqlite_impl.rs, doublets_impl.rs, benched/{mod, sqlite_benched, doublets_benched}.rs}\n     - rust/benches/{bench.rs, benchmarks/{mod, links, each, objects}.rs}\n     - rust/tests/storages.rs, rust/examples/inspect_storage.rs\n     - csharp/{Benchmarks.cs, Program.cs, LinksBenchmarks.cs, LinksSelfTest.cs, TestRun.cs, TestRunResults.cs, SizeAfterCreationColumn.cs, global.json, out.py, test_out.py, results.md, Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets.csproj, .sln, .gitignore}\n     - csharp/Links/{ILinksStorage, LinkRecord, LinksData, LinksStorageFactory, SQLiteLinksStorage, DoubletsLinksStorage}.cs\n     - csharp/Doublets/{DoubletsDbContext, DoubletsTestRun}.cs, csharp/SQLite/{SQLiteDbContext, SQLiteTestRun}.cs, csharp/Model/{BlogPost, BlogPosts}.cs\n     - scripts/{benchmark_report.py, test_benchmark_report.py}\n     - .github/workflows/{rust.yml, CI.yml, cpp.yml, AutoMerge.yml}, .github/dependabot.yml\n     - docs/benchmarks/*.png, README.md, README.ru.md, cpp/conanfile.txt\n   - **rust/src/doublets_impl.rs** (read in full)\n     - Current approach: `DoubletsLinks<S>` with a HashMap char↔symbol cache. Strings are a left-fold of `get_or_create(prev, symbol)`. Markers come from `create_point`. Posts are `create(); update(object, blog_post_marker, object)`; `set_property` uses `get_or_create(object, marker)` then `get_or_create(property, value)`.\n     - This will be rewritten to be generic over T, using C#-matching raw-number symbols and balanced-variant sequences.\n   - **csharp/Links/*.cs** (read)\n     - The current C# links storages use `uint` only, `CreateAndUpdate`, `_links.All(...)`, `Count => QueryAll().Count`.\n     - SQLite currently uses source/target columns, per-call commands and no transactions.\n     - File paths are built with `Path.Combine(Path.GetTempPath(), $\"sqlite-vs-doublets-{Guid.NewGuid():N}-{suffix}\")`.\n   - **csharp/Doublets/DoubletsDbContext.cs** (read): the existing C# object mapping, which serves as the reference structure for markers, converters and the caching decorator.\n   - **External sources cloned for reference**\n     - /tmp/seqsrc: Data.Doublets.Sequences @6a6a69f\n     - /tmp/dsrc: Data.Doublets @f8c521e, version 0.18.1\n     - /tmp/dprobe: probe cargo project\n     - Crate sources: ~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/{doublets-0.5.0, platform-data-2.0.0, platform-mem-0.3.0, platform-num-0.8.0}\n\n4. **Errors and fixes**\n   - `cargo add doublets@0.5.0 rusqlite@0.40.2 --features rusqlite/bundled` failed with \"feature `bundled` activated for crate `rusqlite` but the crate wasn't specified\". Fixed by running separate `cargo add` commands (`cargo add rusqlite@0.40.2 -F bundled`).\n   - matplotlib and numpy are not installed locally. They are needed for the Python report tests and should be installed in the background.\n\n5. **Problem solving**\n   - **Confirmed:**\n     - The nightly pin can be removed.\n     - Both languages can represent strings identically: raw-number symbols with balanced-variant sequences and sequence markers.\n     - Dates can be a single raw number.\n     - The C# `PropertiesOperator` semantics can be mirrored in Rust.\n   - **Rough feasibility estimate:**\n     - Doublets u64 unit storage needs 64 bytes per link (6.4 GB at 100M); u32 needs 3.2 GB.\n     - SQLite at 100M needs about 5–6 GB.\n     - A GitHub ubuntu runner has 4 vCPU, 16 GB RAM and 14 GB of disk.\n     - 100M SQLite file operations could take hours, so 100M may need per-variant