[2026-10-04T16:55:09.194Z] [INFO]       {
[2026-10-04T16:55:09.194Z] [INFO]         "type": "text",
[2026-10-04T16:55:09.194Z] [INFO]         "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. **Primary request and intent**\n   - Solve GitHub issue https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107.\n   - Working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791131286882. Branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b. Draft PR #108 already exists; update it with `gh pr edit` and do not create a new PR.\n   - Push only to issue-107-bce58c21006b. Make atomic commits. Run local checks before pushing.\n   - At the end: merge main if needed, make sure CI passes, update the PR description in the style of merged PR #106, then run `gh pr ready 108`.\n   - Put experiments in ./experiments and examples in ./examples.\n   - Git user is konard. The user's email (link.assistant.team@proton.me) may be used only to identify the user, such as for authorship; it must never be sent to unrelated services.\n\n   **Issue #107 requirements:**\n   - Generate the README results in exactly this hierarchy:\n     ```\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for links\n       Rust doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n       C# doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for objects\n       (same Rust/C# × 32/64-bit structure)\n     ```\n   - SQLite table `links(id, from, to)` with obvious indices on id, from and to; id typed consistently as int or bigint.\n   - Fully symmetric coverage across languages and bit widths.\n   - Both languages need object benchmarks with a sequences cache and without one.\n   - Update all dependencies to their latest versions.\n   - Self-explanatory code: few comments, short, common idioms.\n   - No false positives or false negatives.\n   - Fit the largest sizes possible (100k, 1M, 10M, 100M) within 6-hour CI jobs, splitting jobs if needed. Use fewer repetitions at larger sizes so total work stays roughly constant.\n   - \"Make it perfect.\"\n\n2. **Key technical concepts**\n   - **doublets 0.5.0 on stable Rust (edition 2024, no nightly)**\n     - Re-exports `doublets::data::*`: LinkReference, LinksConstants, Flow, AddrToRaw, RawToAddr, Hybrid.\n     - Re-exports `doublets::mem::{Global, FileMapped}` and `doublets::{unit, split, Doublets, Link}`.\n     - Store constructors: `unit::Store::<T,_>::new(mem)` and `split::Store::<T,_,_>::new(data_mem, index_mem)`.\n     - Doublets trait methods: create, create_point, create_link, update(index,s,t), delete, get_link → Option<Link{index,source,target}>, search, single, get_or_create, each, each_by([any,s,t], |l| Flow::Continue), count, count_by, delete_query_with(query, |_,_| Flow::Continue), constants().any.\n   - **Constants and raw numbers**\n     - The default LinksConstants (internal range 1..=MAX-6) is used on both sides; the C# code also uses the default.\n     - AddrToRaw(x) = MAX − x + 1; RawToAddr inverts it.\n     - Neither store validates existence and neither translates Itself, so markers are built as `m = create(); update(m, root, m)`.\n   - **C# Sequences semantics, mirrored in Rust**\n     - Symbol = GetOrCreate(raw(char utf16), symbolMarker).\n     - BalancedVariant pairs elements layer by layer, carrying the odd last element up.\n     - String sequence = GetOrCreate(balanced, sequenceMarker).\n     - Read: source(seq), walked with the RightSequenceWalker algorithm (push non-elements going to the source; on reaching an element, pop the parent, yield whichever of the parent's source/target are symbols, then continue to the target).\n     - PropertiesOperator: SetValue = get_or_create(obj, prop), delete all (any, op, any), get_or_create(op, value). GetValue = search(obj, prop) followed by single(any, op, any).target.\n     - CachingConverterDecorator corresponds to the two HashMaps: string→seq and seq→string.\n   - **Dataset**\n     - `splitmix64`.\n     - `link(i) = (1 + splitmix64(2i) % i, i + 1 − from)`.\n     - `scattered(n)` is a stride permutation of 1..=n, with the stride = GOLDEN_GAMMA % n adjusted until gcd = 1.\n     - Order-sensitive `mix(a,b) = (a*G ^ b)*G` and `link_checksum = mix(mix(id,from),to)`.\n     - Blog post i: title \"Blog post {i}\", content PARAGRAPHS[i%5] (the five lorem paragraphs from the old C# code), date 1_600_000_000 + splitmix64(i) % 30 days.\n     - Post checksum = byte sums + date.\n   - **Harness**\n     - Each repetition runs a fresh store through the lifecycle, preceded by a discarded warm-up of min(n, 10_000).\n     - Repetitions = max(1, work/size), with default work 10_000_000.\n     - Each timed batch runs in one transaction (SQLite BEGIN/COMMIT) and is validated with a Tally {count, checksum}.\n     - File bytes are measured after create.\n     - JSON output reports median, min, max and samples in ns/op.\n   - **Variants**\n     - Links: SQLite_Memory, SQLite_File, Doublets_{United,Split}_{Volatile,NonVolatile}.\n     - Objects: the same, with Doublets × {_Cached, _Uncached}.\n   - **SQLite schema**\n     - `links(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, \"from\" INTEGER NOT NULL, \"to\" INTEGER NOT NULL)` with indexes `links_from_to(\"from\",\"to\")` and `links_to_from(\"to\",\"from\")`.\n     - `blog_posts(id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, title TEXT, content TEXT, publication_date INTEGER)`.\n     - The same schema is used for both widths.\n   - **Latest versions**\n     - Rust: rusqlite 0.40.2 (bundled, fallible_uint), serde_json 1.0, doublets 0.5.0.\n     - NuGet: Platform.Data.Doublets 0.18.1, Platform.Data.Doublets.Sequences 0.6.5, Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.12.\n     - .NET: net10.0 (local SDK 10.0.112).\n\n3. **Files and code sections**\n   - **Removed with git rm:** rust/benches, rust/src (old), rust/examples, rust/tests, rust/rust-toolchain.toml, rust/out.py, rust/test_out.py, rust/results.md. Cargo.lock was also deleted (it is regenerated by the build).\n   - **rust/rustfmt.toml:** edition changed to 2024. `tab_spaces = 4` kept.\n   - **rust/Cargo.toml:**\n     ```toml\n     [package]\n     name = \"sqlite-vs-doublets\"\n     version = \"0.2.0\"\n     edition = \"2024\"\n     license = \"Unlicense\"\n     description = \"Compares SQLite and Doublets as storage for links and for objects\"\n     [dependencies]\n     doublets = \"0.5.0\"\n     rusqlite = { version = \"0.40.2\", features = [\"bundled\", \"fallible_uint\"] }\n     serde_json = \"1.0\"\n     [profile.release]\n     lto = true\n     codegen-units = 1\n     ```\n   - **rust/src/lib.rs:** `pub mod dataset; pub mod harness; pub mod links; pub mod objects;`\n   - **rust/src/dataset.rs**\n     - Functions: splitmix64, link, mix, link_checksum, `links_checksum(n, swapped)` (wrapping fold), scattered, gcd.\n     - `BlogPost {title, content, publication_date: u64}` with `checksum()`.\n     - PARAGRAPHS[5], `blog_post(i)`, `blog_posts_checksum(n)`, `blog_posts_by_number_checksum(n)` = Σ mix(i, checksum).\n   - **rust/src/links.rs**\n     - `Link<T>{id,from,to}`.\n     - `trait LinksStorage<T>`: create, update, delete, get, search, each, each_with_from, each_with_to, count, and `transaction<R>(&mut self, work: impl FnOnce(&mut Self)->R)` (default: just runs the work).\n     - `SqliteLinks<T>`: open(path) and in_memory(); uses prepare_cached; `sql()`/`id()` i64 helpers; `each_where` helper; transaction does BEGIN/COMMIT.\n     - `DoubletsLinks<T, D>{store, id_type}` with `new(store)`; implements the trait through `self.store.*`, using each_by([any,from,any]) and [any,any,to].\n   - **rust/src/objects.rs**\n     - `trait BlogPostsStorage<T>`: create(&BlogPost)->T, get(&mut, id)->Option<BlogPost>, each(&mut, FnMut(T, BlogPost)), delete, count(&mut), transaction.\n     - `SqliteBlogPosts<T>`.\n     - `DoubletsBlogPosts<T, D>`:\n       - Fields: unicode_symbol, unicode_sequence, title, content, publication_date and blog_post markers, plus `cache: Option<SequencesCache<T>>`.\n       - `new(links, cache_sequences)` creates the meaning root with create_point, then markers via create + update(m, root, m).\n       - Methods: sequence (with cache), balanced_variant (chunks(2) layers), string (with cache; walk; RawToAddr), walk (C# walker port), source, target, set_property, property, delete_property.\n       - Post creation: create, then update(post, blog_post_marker, post), then set the properties; the date is stored as AddrToRaw(date).\n       - Delete removes the value link and the object-property link for each property, then the post.\n       - each collects ids from [any, blog_post, any], then gets each post.\n       - count uses count_by.\n   - **rust/src/harness.rs**\n     - Constants LINKS_VARIANTS[6], OBJECTS_VARIANTS[10], WARM_UP_SIZE = 10_000; `type Timings`.\n     - `Tally{count, checksum}` with add, add_link and of.\n     - Helpers: `timed(timings, op, expected: Tally, work)` (asserts after timing), `int`, `uint`.\n     - `links_lifecycle<T, S: LinksStorage<T>>(storage, n, created: &mut dyn FnMut()) -> Timings`:\n       - Operations: create, query_all, query_by_id, query_by_from_to, query_by_from, query_by_to, update (swap from/to; then an untimed check that a full scan matches the swapped checksum), delete (then assert count == 0).\n     - `objects_lifecycle<T, S: BlogPostsStorage<T>>`: create (collects ids), read_all, read_by_id (scattered, mix(i, checksum)), delete, then assert count == 0.\n     - `Measurement{variant, file_bytes, operations}` with `to_json` (median_ns, min_ns, max_ns, samples_ns).\n     - `measure<S>(variant, n, repetitions, directory, open: impl Fn(&Path)->S, lifecycle: fn(&mut S,u64,&mut dyn FnMut())->Timings)`: per-repetition workspace dir `{variant}-{rep}`, removed afterwards; prints an eprintln summary per repetition.\n     - `measure_links<T>` and `measure_objects<T>` match the variant name to a store constructor; objects strip the _Cached/_Uncached suffix.\n     - File names: links.db, blog_posts.db, links.links, data.links, index.links.\n   - **rust/src/main.rs**\n     - CLI: `sqlite-vs-doublets <links|objects> <32|64> <size> [--work N | --repetitions N] [--variants A,B] [--directory DIR] [--output FILE]`. DEFAULT_WORK is 10_000_000.\n     - JSON report fields: language \"Rust\", category, bits, size, repetitions, warm_up_size, sqlite_version, results.\n   - **Reference sources:** /tmp/seqsrc (C# Sequences), /tmp/dsrc (C# Doublets 0.18.1), crate sources in ~/.cargo/registry/src/index.crates.io-1949cf8c6b5b557f/.\n   - **Not yet modified:** csharp/*, scripts/benchmark_report.py and its test, .github/workflows/{rust.yml (still uses nightly, Criterion and out.py — must be rewritten), CI.yml, cpp.yml}, README.md, README.ru.md.\n\n4. **Errors and fixes**\n   - `cargo add` with `--features rusqlite/bundled` failed in the earlier session. Fixed by running separate `cargo add` commands.\n   - **E0119 conflicting implementations:** the blanket `impl LinksStorage<T> for D: Doublets<T>` conflicted with SqliteLinks. Fixed by adding the `DoubletsLinks<T, D>` wrapper with PhantomData.\n   - **E0308 closure type mismatch** in measure_links and measure_objects, because the lifecycle closure was inferred for the first store type. Fixed by making the lifecycles generic fns (`created: &mut dyn FnMut()`) passed as fn pointers.\n   - **u64 ToSql/FromSql not implemented.** Fixed by adding the rusqlite `fallible_uint` feature.\n   - **`T::from(char)` invalid** for a generic LinkReference. Changed to `T::try_from(char).unwrap()`.\n   - **Current runtime error:** the links smoke runs at both 32 and 64 bits panic in doublets-0.5.0/src/mem/split/generic/internal_sources_recursion_less_tree.rs:153 with \"index out of bounds: the len is 1040384 but the index is 1048575\". Objects runs completed, at least for the Split variants shown.\n\n5. **Problem solving**\n   - **Smoke results (n=1000, 2 repetitions, objects):**\n     - Doublets_Split_NonVolatile_Cached: create ~5.3–5.9 µs/op, read_all ~1.4 µs, read_by_id ~0.55 µs, delete ~2.3 µs.\n     - Uncached: create ~80–86 µs, read ~20–25 µs, delete ~2.5 µs.\n     - The uncached create cost is high, so object sizes will need to be smaller than link sizes.\n   - **Open:** the split-store panic in the links lifecycle.\n     - Suspected causes: deleting in scattered order, update/delete in the split store, or the warm-up path.\n     - Needs RUST_BACKTRACE=1 and per-variant runs (`--variants Doublets_Split_Volatile`) to isolate the variant and operation.\n     - It may be a doublets-rs bug. If so, document or report it (possibly in an experiment script) and work around it if possible, for example by checking whether it only happens with the unit store or the split store.\n   - **Still to verify:** correctness tests against SQLite results; whether the 64-bit objects run passed for all variants (only the tail of the output was seen).\n\n6. **All user messages**\n   - \"Issue to solve: https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107 / Your prepared branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b / Your prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791131286882 / Your prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/pull/108 / Proceed.\"\n   - Security and usage constraint (system context): the user's email link.assistant.team@proton.me is to be used only to identify the user, such as for authorship and attribution; it must never be sent to an unrelated service, such as in a request header, URL or payload, unless the user explicitly asks.\n   - Periodic system prompts asked for brief status updates (\"say in a few words what you're doing, then continue\").\n\n7. **Pending tasks**\n   - Fix or diagnose the Doublets split-store panic in the links lifecycle. Then add Rust integration tests (tests/storages.rs) that check every variant against the expected values, including exact BlogPost round-trip equality, and check that Double