[2026-10-04T17:08:19.677Z] [INFO]         "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. **Primary request and intent**\n   - Solve GitHub issue https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107.\n   - Working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791131286882. Branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b.\n   - Draft PR #108 already exists: update it with `gh pr edit` and never create a new PR.\n   - Push only to issue-107-bce58c21006b. Make atomic commits and run local checks before pushing.\n   - Finishing steps:\n     - Merge main if needed.\n     - Make sure CI passes.\n     - Check the latest issue and PR comments.\n     - Update the PR description in the style of merged PR #106.\n     - Run `gh pr ready 108`.\n   - Put experiments in ./experiments and examples in ./examples.\n   - Git user is konard. The user's email (link.assistant.team@proton.me) may be used only to identify the user, such as for authorship; it must never be sent to unrelated services.\n\n   **Issue #107 requirements (verbatim essence):**\n   - README results generated exactly in this hierarchy:\n     ```\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for links\n       Rust doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n       C# doublets vs SQLite\n         32 bit address/id space benchmarks\n         64 bit address/id space benchmarks\n     Doublets vs SQLite as storage for objects\n       (same Rust/C# × 32/64-bit structure)\n     ```\n   - SQLite table `links(id, from, to)` with obvious indices; id typed the same (int or bigint) in C# and Rust.\n   - Fully symmetrical, even coverage.\n   - Object storage benchmarks with and without a sequences cache, in all languages.\n   - All dependencies updated to their latest versions.\n   - Self-explanatory, short, precise code with few comments.\n   - No false positives or false negatives.\n   - Fit the largest data sizes possible (100k, 1M, 10M, 100M) in the 6-hour CI window, splitting CI per benchmark if needed. Do fewer repetitions at larger sizes so total work is about the same; repeat 100k more, \"but not too much, so we don't waste resources\".\n   - Follow internet best practices. Use the most common ways to use SQLite and Doublets in each language.\n   - \"Make it perfect, please.\"\n\n2. **Key technical concepts**\n   - **doublets 0.5.0 (stable Rust, edition 2024)**\n     - Store constructors: `unit::Store::<T,_>::new(mem)` and `split::Store::<T,_,_>::new(data, index)`; memory is `Global::new()` or `FileMapped::from_path`.\n     - Doublets API: create, create_point, create_link, update, delete, get_link, search, single, get_or_create, each, each_by, count_by, delete_query_with, constants().any.\n     - Number conversions: AddrToRaw / RawToAddr; `T::from_byte(0)`.\n     - Split stores expose `get_data_part`, `get_index_part` and `is_unused` publicly.\n   - **Split store bug (doublets 0.5.0)**\n     - `update_links` calls `is_virtual` (which is `is_unused`) after detaching the link, so a link updated to reference itself goes into the external trees.\n     - A later delete then panics with \"index out of bounds: the len is 1040384 but the index is 1048575\" in `detach_core`.\n     - Workaround: update the link to (0, 0) first when the new pair references the link itself.\n     - Duplicate links are unsupported by design. The source tree is keyed by target, so equal keys corrupt it.\n   - **C# empty-string convention:** an empty string maps to the unicode sequence marker, and the converter returns String.Empty for the marker. BalancedVariant of an empty list returns zero; of one element, returns that element.\n   - **Dataset:** `link(i)` gives unique pairs because from + to = i + 1, so swapping never creates duplicates. Self-references occur when from == 1 or from == i.\n   - **Harness:** fresh store per repetition, warm-up of min(n, 10k), one transaction per timed batch, every batch validated by a count/checksum Tally, JSON output.\n   - **Timing estimates so far**\n     - Links 100k (64-bit): all 6 variants in 16 s per repetition, about 160 µs per link for the full set.\n     - Objects 10k: 20 s per repetition, about 1 ms per post for the full set. Uncached Doublets create is about 90–150 µs/op.\n     - GitHub runners: 4 CPUs, 16 GB RAM, about 14 GB disk, 6-hour job limit. The local machine has 6 CPUs and 11 GB RAM.\n\n3. **Files and code sections**\n   - **Committed in 229ae26** (\"Rewrite Rust benchmarks on stable doublets 0.5.0 and rusqlite 0.40\"):\n     - The new rust/src: lib.rs, dataset.rs, links.rs, objects.rs, harness.rs, main.rs.\n     - rust/tests/storages.rs, rust/Cargo.toml, Cargo.lock, rustfmt.toml (edition 2024).\n     - The legacy Rust files were deleted.\n     - experiments/split_store_delete was added, and `target/` was added to the root .gitignore.\n   - **rust/src/links.rs, `DoubletsLinks::update`:**\n     ```rust\n     fn update(&mut self, id: T, from: T, to: T) {\n         // doublets 0.5.0 split stores lose a link updated to reference itself unless it starts\n         // from the empty state `create_link` uses, see experiments/split_store_delete\n         if from == id || to == id {\n             self.store.update(id, T::from_byte(0), T::from_byte(0)).unwrap();\n         }\n         self.store.update(id, from, to).unwrap();\n     }\n     ```\n   - **rust/src/objects.rs**\n     - `sequence()`: `if string.is_empty() { return self.unicode_sequence; }`\n     - `string()`: `if sequence == self.unicode_sequence { return String::new(); }`\n     - `balanced_variant` returns `layer[0]`.\n   - **rust/tests/storages.rs** contains 7 tests, all passing:\n     - `links_are_unique_and_reference_created_ids`\n     - `scattered_visits_every_id_once` (n in 1, 2, 3, 10, 1000, 1024, 65536)\n     - `every_links_variant_passes_the_lifecycle` (u32 and u64, SIZE = 1000, via `harness::measure_links`)\n     - `every_objects_variant_passes_the_lifecycle` (SIZE / 10)\n     - `split_store_keeps_links_updated_to_reference_themselves`\n     - `lifecycle_rejects_a_storage_that_loses_records`: a should_panic test with a Lossy wrapper whose `each` only returns links from 1, expecting \"query_all returned unexpected records\".\n     - `every_objects_storage_returns_the_stored_posts`: exact round trip including a Unicode/emoji title and an empty title, for SQLite and all four Doublets kinds × cached/uncached × u32/u64.\n     - The `tempdir(name)` helper uses the temp dir plus the process id.\n   - **rust/src/main.rs**\n     - Currently `DEFAULT_WORK = 10_000_000` and `repetitions = (work/size).max(1)`.\n     - Planned change: cap the repetitions, for example `clamp(work/size, 1, ~10)`, with a smaller default work such as 3M.\n   - **experiments/split_store_delete/src/main.rs**\n     - The doc comment explains the root cause.\n     - Six scenarios of (op, id, from, to) steps; usage is `<unit|split|dump> <scenario index>`, and exit code 101 means the panic.\n     - The split store panics on scenarios 0 (\"point: update to itself, delete\") and 2 (\"swap a link that references itself, delete\"). The unit store passes them all.\n   - **experiments/sizing/run.sh** (not yet committed)\n     - `run.sh <links|objects> <32|64> <size> [extra]` runs one repetition of every variant.\n     - It writes /tmp/sizing-*.log and .json and prints the elapsed seconds plus the per-variant `#1` lines.\n   - **Existing C# files (not yet modified)**\n     - csharp/: Benchmarks.cs, Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets.csproj/.sln, Doublets/*, Links/* (DoubletsLinksStorage, ILinksStorage, LinkRecord, LinksData, LinksStorageFactory, SQLiteLinksStorage), LinksBenchmarks.cs, LinksSelfTest.cs, Model/BlogPost.cs, Model/BlogPosts.cs, Program.cs, SQLite/*, SizeAfterCreationColumn.cs, TestRun.cs, TestRunResults.cs, global.json (sdk 8.0.100), out.py, results.md, test_out.py.\n     - The csproj targets net8.0 and uses BenchmarkDotNet 0.13.6, Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite 7.0.9, Platform.Diagnostics 0.1.0, Doublets 0.17.2 and Sequences 0.5.2, with NoWarn NETSDK1206 and Debug project references.\n   - **Other files**\n     - .github/workflows: AutoMerge.yml, CI.yml, cpp.yml, rust.yml. rust.yml still uses nightly, Criterion and out.py and must be rewritten.\n     - .github/dependabot.yml, scripts/benchmark_report.py and its test, docs/benchmarks/*.png, README.md, README.ru.md.\n\n4. **Errors and fixes**\n   - **Split store panic:** fixed with the conditional reset-to-(0,0) workaround in `update`, plus a regression test. Diagnosed with a dump of the index parts.\n   - **Misdiagnosed scenarios:** my first scenarios created duplicate links, which made the split store fail without any update and made the unit store hang. These were invalid scenarios, not bugs, and were rewritten without duplicates.\n   - **Empty string round trip:** panicked at objects.rs:209 (`get_link(0)`). Fixed by using the marker for empty strings, as C# does.\n   - **`/usr/bin/time` missing:** used date arithmetic in run.sh instead.\n   - **Blocked sleep:** a `sleep 100 && cat` command was blocked by the harness. Use Monitor or until-loops, or background tasks, instead.\n\n5. **Problem solving**\n   - **Smoke-run observation:** the Doublets NonVolatile file_bytes value is the preallocated mmap size (32–40 MB at n = 1000). Undecided whether to keep reporting it.\n   - **Links 100k observation:** Doublets United lookups take about 1 µs because of random tree access, which seems genuine. The United update costs about 6 µs.\n   - **Size planning (tentative)**\n     - Links: 100k, 1M and 10M, one job per language × bits × size.\n     - 100M is probably infeasible because of memory and time (SQLite memory and Doublets volatile at about 6–13 GB peak), unless split per variant. Whatever doesn't fit must be documented.\n     - Objects: likely 10k, 100k and 1M, since 10M would mean about 8 GB of in-memory SQLite and about 3 hours per repetition.\n   - The 1M links sizing result is still pending.\n\n6. **All user messages**\n   - \"Issue to solve: https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107 / Your prepared branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b / Your prepared working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791131286882 / Your prepared Pull Request: https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/pull/108 / Proceed.\"\n   - Security and usage constraint (system context): the user's email link.assistant.team@proton.me is to be used only to identify the user, such as for authorship and attribution; it must never be sent to an unrelated service (request header, URL or payload) unless the user explicitly asks.\n   - Periodic system prompts asked for short status updates (\"say in a few words what you're doing, then continue\").\n\n7. **Pending tasks**\n   - Finish sizing: read the 1M links result from /tmp/sizing-links.out and /tmp/sizing-links-64-1000000.log; optionally try 10M links and 100k objects. Choose sizes, the work budget and the repetition cap, then update main.rs (cap the repetitions). Commit experiments/sizing.\n   - **C# rewrite:**\n     - Target net10.0 (local SDK 10.0.112) and update global.json.\n     - Use Platform.Data.Doublets 0.18.1, Platform.Data.Doublets.Sequences 0.6.5 and Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.12.\n     - Remove EF Core, BenchmarkDotNet, Platform.Diagnostics and the legacy files (including csharp/out.py, test_out.py and results.md if superseded).\n     - Make it generic over uint/ulong, with the same dataset (splitmix64, link, scattered, mix, blog_post with the five PARAGRAPHS), harness, variants, validation, CLI and JSON format (language \"C#\").\n     - Use the Sequences converters (BalancedVariantConverter, CachingConverterDecorator, PropertiesOperator, RightSequenceWalker, Unicode converters with Unindex) with the same markers.\n     - Add tests, and check whether C# split memory handles self-reference updates.\n   - **Unified Python report** (scripts/benchmark_report.py and its tests): merge results/*.json into the README.md and README.ru.md sections in the required hierarchy, with tables per size and charts in docs/benchmarks. Install matplotlib and numpy in the background.\n   - **Workflows:**\n     - A benchmarks matrix (language × bits × category × size, each job ≤ 6h) that uploads JSON, plus a report job that commits results.\n     - Rewrite rust.yml (stable toolchain; fmt, clippy, test, smoke run) and CI.yml for C#.\n     - Add cargo to dependabot; consider removing cpp.yml.\n   - Possibly report the doublets-rs split-store bug upstream. This is an outward-facing action, so for now document it in the PR instead.\n   - Push, check CI, update the PR #108 title and description (design choices, the bug workaround, sizes that fit and limits), then run `gh pr ready 108`.\n\n8. **Current work**\n   - Planning benchmark sizes from the sizing runs and starting the C# rewrite.\n   - The last action listed the C# files and showed the old csproj (net8.0, BenchmarkDotNet, EF Core 7.0.9, Doublets 0.17.2, Sequences 0.5.2) and global.json (8.0.100).\n   - The background links sizing run, `(./run.sh links 64 100000 && ./run.sh links 64 1000000) > /tmp/sizing-links.out`, had finished 100k (16 s) and was running 1M.\n\n9. **Next step**\n   - Check /tmp/sizing-links.out for the 1M links timing.\n   - Decide `DEFAULT_WORK` and the repetition cap in rust/src/main.rs, then commit experiments/sizing/run.sh together with that change.\n   - Then begin the C# rewrite: replace the csproj with net10.0 and the latest packages, delete the legacy files, and write the C# equivalents of dataset, links, objects, harness and Program, mirroring the Rust code and JSON format.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /home/box/.claude/projects/-tmp-gh-issue-solver-1791131286882/78aa02d9-2054-4be5-9990-31ae739e08df.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."
